1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,480
A product page is hot. The database is
not. Someone says "add a cache." Caches
buy latency with correctness risk — and
invalidation is the boss fight. Without an
invalidation story, you have a faster way
to be wrong.

2
00:00:12,599 --> 00:00:25,640
Local caches live in-process — fast,
simple, per instance. Distributed caches
shared across instances avoid each node
reloading the same miss, at the cost of
network and operational complexity.

3
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:35,839
A local Caffeine cache may be enough for
read-mostly data with short TTL on each
pod. A Redis-style cache may be needed
when hit rate must be shared.

4
00:00:35,959 --> 00:00:46,399
Local caches go stale independently after
a write to another instance unless you
broadcast invalidations — acceptable for
short-TTL display data, unacceptable for
permissions.

5
00:00:46,519 --> 00:00:56,439
Distributed caches add a dependency that
can be down; your resilience story must
include cache-miss fallback without
melting the database. Look at this java
for a moment.

6
00:00:56,559 --> 00:01:08,679
Size bounds and TTL or TTI eviction keep
the cache from becoming Episode Fifty-
Seven's unbounded map. Soft references
alone are not a substitute — Episode
Sixty-One warned.

7
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:20,000
Cache-aside means the app checks the
cache, loads on miss, then populates.
Read-through means the cache library loads
via a loader — as above. Keep the pattern
consistent across the codebase.

8
00:01:20,119 --> 00:01:29,999
Invalidation is the boss fight because
freshness and hit rate pull opposite
directions. Short TTL increases misses;
long TTL increases staleness.

9
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:38,399
Delete-on-write keeps freshness for that
key but needs every write path to
cooperate — including admin tools and
batch jobs that bypass the app.

10
00:01:38,519 --> 00:01:51,999
Before shipping, write the invalidation
sentence in the PR: "On product update, we
delete key product:{id}; TTL is five
minutes as a backstop; loader is single-
flight." If you cannot write that
sentence,

11
00:01:52,119 --> 00:01:53,799
the cache is not designed yet.

12
00:01:53,919 --> 00:02:08,159
Stampede control matters when a popular
key expires and a hundred requests miss
together. Single-flight loading — like
LoadingCache's loader — slightly staggered
TTLs, or soft values reduce the thundering
herd.

13
00:02:08,279 --> 00:02:13,479
Measure hit rate and staleness together. A
high hit rate serving wrong data is not a
win.

14
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:24,399
If hit rate is high and origin load is
still high, dig into miss traces before
growing the cache cluster — you may be
caching the wrong grain or suffering
stampedes at expiry.

15
00:02:24,519 --> 00:02:35,159
Caching non-idempotent results blindly is
how duplicates appear. Caching a POST
response that creates a seat reservation
means a retry can return success without a
seat.

16
00:02:35,279 --> 00:02:47,119
Separate read models you cache from write
side effects you do not. Cache keys should
follow resource identifiers clients
already understand — and those identifiers
live inside longer-lived API contracts.

17
00:02:47,239 --> 00:02:56,639
Personalized responses demand extra care
for privacy and correctness. Hardest cache
problem? Invalidation and stampede under
concurrent misses.

18
00:02:56,759 --> 00:03:08,599
Say that, then mention bounds, TTL, and
explicit delete-on-write as the ordinary
tools that prevent heroic outages. Caches
buy latency with correctness risk — keep
saying both halves.

19
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:20,919
APIs that sit in front of caches and
databases need long-term contracts —
idempotency, errors, and compatibility —
or every cache win becomes a client
migration tax.
